        "message" : [
          "%md",
          "",
          "**Community Partner Network with Property Graph**",
          "",
          "This notebook shows how a public-service team can use a property graph to understand how programs, partner organizations, and handoffs connect. The `INFLUENCER_NETWORK` graph models a public-service program such as Benefits Eligibility, the community partner that promotes it, and organizations connected through `CONNECTS_TO` handoffs.",
          "",
          "In this workshop's graph model, a `brand` vertex represents a public-service program and an `influencer` vertex represents a community partner. The labels come from the graph objects, while the learner-facing question is about finding useful coordination paths between programs and partners.",
          "",
          "Run each graph paragraph with its play button, then use the visual result to discuss coordination evidence. A connection is a lead for human review; it is not proof of eligibility, service quality, or authorization to refer."
        ],
        "selectedVisualization" : "html",
        "visualizationConfig" : null,
        "hideCode" : false,
        "hideResult" : false,
        "hideGutter" : true,
        "hideVizConfig" : false,
        "hideInIFrame" : false,
        "enabled" : true,
        "forms" : "[]",
        "result" : {
          "startTime" : 1788699724065,
          "endTime" : 1788699724105,
          "interpreter" : "md",
          "taskStatus" : "SUCCESS",
          "status" : "SUCCESS",
          "results" : [
            {
              "message" : "<p><strong>Community Partner Network with Property Graph</strong></p>\n<p>This notebook shows how a public-service team can use a property graph to understand how programs, partner organizations, and handoffs connect. The <code>INFLUENCER_NETWORK</code> graph models a public-service program such as Benefits Eligibility, the community partner that promotes it, and organizations connected through <code>CONNECTS_TO</code> handoffs.</p>\n<p>In this workshop's graph model, a <code>brand</code> vertex represents a public-service program and an <code>influencer</code> vertex represents a community partner. The labels come from the graph objects, while the learner-facing question is about finding useful coordination paths between programs and partners.</p>\n<p>Run each graph paragraph with its play button, then use the visual result to discuss coordination evidence. A connection is a lead for human review; it is not proof of eligibility, service quality, or authorization to refer.</p>\n",
              "type" : "HTML"
            }
          ],
          "forms" : "[]"
        },
        "relations" : [ ],
        "dynamicFormParams" : "{}"
      },
      {
        "row" : 0,
        "col" : 0,
        "sizeX" : 0,
        "width" : 0,
        "title" : null,
        "hasTitle" : false,
        "message" : [
          "%md",
          "**Visual exercise 1: direct partner handoffs**",
          "",
          "This first graph query starts at the `Benefits Eligibility` program and follows the path to a source partner and then to a connected partner. Read the `MATCH` pattern from left to right: the program is connected to the source through a `promotes` relationship, and the source reaches the partner through a `connects_to` handoff.",
          "",
          "The `WHERE` clause anchors the search on one program, while `COLUMNS` returns the vertex and edge identifiers that Graph Studio needs to draw the result. Look for the program, the starting partner, the connected partners, and the relationship types that join them. This view helps a coordinator see which organizations are already connected before planning outreach."
        ],
        "selectedVisualization" : "html",
        "visualizationConfig" : null,
        "hideCode" : false,
        "hideResult" : false,
        "hideGutter" : true,
        "hideVizConfig" : false,
        "hideInIFrame" : false,
        "enabled" : true,
        "forms" : "[]",
        "result" : {
          "startTime" : 1788699756289,
          "endTime" : 1788699756359,
          "interpreter" : "md",
          "taskStatus" : "SUCCESS",
          "status" : "SUCCESS",
          "results" : [
            {
              "message" : "<p><strong>Visual exercise 1: direct partner handoffs</strong></p>\n<p>This first graph query starts at the <code>Benefits Eligibility</code> program and follows the path to a source partner and then to a connected partner. Read the <code>MATCH</code> pattern from left to right: the program is connected to the source through a <code>promotes</code> relationship, and the source reaches the partner through a <code>connects_to</code> handoff.</p>\n<p>The <code>WHERE</code> clause anchors the search on one program, while <code>COLUMNS</code> returns the vertex and edge identifiers that Graph Studio needs to draw the result. Look for the program, the starting partner, the connected partners, and the relationship types that join them. This view helps a coordinator see which organizations are already connected before planning outreach.</p>\n",
              "type" : "HTML"
            }
          ],
          "forms" : "[]"
        },
        "relations" : [ ],
        "dynamicFormParams" : "{}"
      },
      {
        "row" : 0,
        "col" : 0,
        "sizeX" : 0,
        "width" : 0,
        "title" : null,
        "hasTitle" : false,
        "message" : [
          "%sql",
          "SELECT *",
          "FROM GRAPH_TABLE (",
          "  influencer_network",
          "    MATCH (program IS brand)<-[program_link IS promotes]-(source IS influencer)-[handoff IS connects_to]->(partner IS influencer)",
          "      WHERE program.brand_name = 'Benefits Eligibility'",
          "        COLUMNS (",
          "            vertex_id(program) AS program_id,",
          "                vertex_id(source) AS source_partner_id,",
          "                    edge_id(program_link) AS program_link_id,",
          "                        edge_id(handoff) AS handoff_id,",
          "                            vertex_id(partner) AS connected_partner_id",
          "                              )",
          "                              );"
        ],
        "selectedVisualization" : "visualization",
