          "results" : [
            {
              "message" : "<p><strong>Visual exercise 2: two-hop coordination path</strong></p>\n<p>Now start with Colorado Benefits Network (<code>@co-benefits</code>) and follow two <code>connects_to</code> edges. Read the <code>MATCH</code> pattern as a three-part path: <code>source</code> is the starting partner, <code>middle</code> is the intermediary, and <code>destination</code> is the organization reached through the second handoff.</p>\n<p>The <code>WHERE</code> clause keeps the investigation focused on one known partner, and <code>COLUMNS</code> returns the source, intermediary, destination, and both handoff identifiers for the graph visualization. Use the intermediary and destination to discuss a focused coordination conversation; the path is evidence to review, not an automatic referral or eligibility decision.</p>\n",
              "type" : "HTML"
            }
          ],
          "forms" : "[]"
        },
        "relations" : [ ],
        "dynamicFormParams" : "{}"
      },
      {
        "row" : 0,
        "col" : 0,
        "sizeX" : 0,
        "width" : 0,
        "title" : null,
        "hasTitle" : false,
        "message" : [
          "%sql",
          "SELECT *",
          "FROM GRAPH_TABLE (",
          "  influencer_network",
          "    MATCH (source IS influencer)-[first_edge IS connects_to]->(middle IS influencer)-[second_edge IS connects_to]->(destination IS influencer)",
          "      WHERE source.handle = '@co-benefits'",
          "        COLUMNS (",
          "            vertex_id(source) AS source_partner_id,",
          "                edge_id(first_edge) AS first_handoff_id,",
          "                    vertex_id(middle) AS intermediary_partner_id,",
          "                        edge_id(second_edge) AS second_handoff_id,",
          "                            vertex_id(destination) AS destination_partner_id",
          "                              )",
          "                              );"
        ],
